# AWS Credentials Template
# 
# This file shows the structure for AWS credentials needed by the Spark application.
# 
# SETUP INSTRUCTIONS:
# 
# 1. Copy this file:
#    cp aws_credentials.template aws_credentials
# 
# 2. Edit aws_credentials and replace placeholders:
#    Line 1: Your AWS Access Key ID (e.g., AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE)
#    Line 2: Your AWS Secret Access Key (e.g., wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY)
# 
# 3. Secure the file:
#    chmod 600 aws_credentials
# 
# 4. NEVER commit aws_credentials to git!
#    It should already be in .gitignore
# 
# EXAMPLE (DO NOT USE THESE KEYS):
# AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE
# wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY
#
# FORMAT:
# Line 1: AWS Access Key ID (no spaces, no quotes, no comments)
# Line 2: AWS Secret Access Key (no spaces, no quotes, no comments)
#
# WHERE TO FIND YOUR CREDENTIALS:
# 1. Log in to AWS Console
# 2. Click your name (top right) → Security Credentials
# 3. Scroll to "Access keys" section
# 4. Click "Create access key"
# 5. Copy both keys (you can only see the secret once!)
#
# IMPORTANT SECURITY NOTES:
# - Never share these credentials
# - Never commit them to version control
# - Rotate them regularly (every 90 days)
# - Use IAM roles instead when possible
# - Limit permissions to only what's needed:
#   * s3:GetObject
#   * s3:PutObject
#   * s3:ListBucket
#   * emr:DescribeCluster
#   * emr:AddJobFlowSteps
#
# TROUBLESHOOTING:
# If Spark can't read credentials:
# - Check file exists: ls -la src/main/resources/aws_credentials
# - Check permissions: chmod 600 src/main/resources/aws_credentials
# - Check format: no blank lines, no spaces, exactly 2 lines
# - Rebuild project: sbt clean package
#
# For more help, see README.md "Configuration" section
